@lesson study-plan
Q: Which habits make study stick? (select all)
* Re-deriving a key result from memory each week
* Building a checkpoint project at the end of each phase
* Writing a short README or post explaining each project
- Reading every lesson twice without doing exercises
> Active recall and building beat re-reading.

Q: According to the roadmap, which course should a newcomer to programming start with?
* Python for AI
- Large Language Models
- Systems & Scale
- Generative Models
> Everything else builds on fluent Python.

Q: Consistency matters more than intensity when following a long study plan.
= true
> Regular moderate effort compounds; streaks help build the habit.

@lesson interviews
Q: What does STAR stand for in behavioural interviews?
* Situation, Task, Action, Result
- Skills, Tools, Achievements, References
- Start, Talk, Answer, Repeat
- Strategy, Teamwork, Analysis, Results
> Structure each story this way and quantify the result.

Q: In a coding interview, what should you do before writing code?
* Restate the problem, ask about constraints and edge cases, and outline a brute-force approach
- Start coding immediately
- Ask for the solution
- Optimize prematurely
> Communication is graded as much as the final code.

Q: Your training loss isn't decreasing. Which checks come first? (select all)
* Whether the model can overfit a single small batch
* Whether the initial loss is about \(\ln C\)
* Whether inputs and labels are aligned
- Adding more regularization
> Rule out bugs before tuning.

Q: Why is ROC-AUC potentially misleading on heavily imbalanced data?
* The false positive rate divides by a huge number of negatives, so many false alarms still look small
- ROC-AUC cannot be computed for imbalanced data
- It is always 0.5
- It ignores positives
> Use PR curves to see precision directly.

@lesson portfolio
Q: Which project attributes impress hiring managers most? (select all)
* A real problem with real data
* A baseline and an honest evaluation
* A deployed demo with a clear write-up
- Many small tutorial notebooks
> Three excellent projects beat thirty tutorials.

Q: Which CV bullet is strongest?
* "Cut p95 latency 40% by quantizing and batching the ranking model"
- "Worked on machine learning"
- "Familiar with PyTorch, TensorFlow, JAX, Keras"
- "Responsible for various AI tasks"
> Specific impact with numbers beats lists of tools.

Q: Projects in under-served areas (for example Arabic or Darija NLP) can stand out because they are harder to copy.
= true
> Differentiation is valuable in a crowded field.

@lesson papers
Q: In the three-pass method, what do you do in the first pass?
* Read the title, abstract, figures and conclusion to decide relevance
- Re-derive every equation
- Re-implement the code
- Read the references
> Five minutes is enough to decide whether to go deeper.

Q: A paper reports a 0.3-point gain without error bars over three seeds. What should you ask? (select all)
* Is the gain larger than seed-to-seed variance?
* Were the baselines tuned as carefully?
* What did the improvement cost in compute?
- Nothing; all published results are reliable
> Small gains are often within noise.

Q: Re-implementing the core idea of a paper in a small notebook is where real understanding happens.
= true
> Implementation exposes every detail the text glosses over.

@lesson exam
Q: In an ML system design interview, you are asked to design spam detection. What do you do first?
* Clarify scale, latency, cost of errors and available labels
- Propose a Transformer immediately
- Start writing SQL
- Discuss GPU types
> Requirements shape every later choice.

Q: Which statements about the AI engineer interview loop are generally true? (select all)
* It usually includes coding and ML fundamentals
* System design matters more for senior roles
* Project deep-dives test real ownership
- Only a degree matters
> Prepare each round deliberately.

Q: What makes a strong answer to "explain a project you are proud of"?
* The problem, your specific decisions, what failed, and measured results
- A list of technologies used
- How long it took
- Only the final accuracy
> Interviewers look for judgement and ownership.

Q: Reading papers is more valuable when you reproduce at least one key result.
= true
> Reproduction is the best test of understanding.
